Third-party notices for wpantry
===============================

wpantry is Pantry's command line (https://github.com/waffuruai/pantry): MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 Waffuru AI Inc. Its release binaries are built from the Rust
crates below and carry the data and fonts listed after them, whose licences ask
that these notices travel with every copy. `wpantry licenses` prints this file,
and each release ships it as THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt.

Generated by `mise run notices` (cargo-about, rust/about.toml and
rust/about.hbs); don't edit it by hand.


Crates
------

Each crate a release binary is built from, for any target, and its declared
licence. Where a crate offers a choice, the text below is the one we use.
Pantry's own crates (wpantry, wpantry-cli, wpantry-yara) have no version here:
they are the release's.

{{#each crates}}
  {{{package.name}}}{{#if package.source}} {{{package.version}}}{{/if}}: {{{license}}}
{{/each}}


Licence texts
-------------
{{#each licenses}}

==== {{{name}}} ({{{id}}})
Used by: {{#each used_by}}{{{crate.name}}}{{#if crate.source}} {{{crate.version}}}{{/if}}{{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}

{{{text}}}
{{/each}}


Other material
--------------

What the binary carries that is not a crate.

- The Rust standard library (std, core, alloc), compiled into every binary:
  MIT or Apache-2.0, at the user's option; its MIT licence follows.
- musl libc, statically linked into the Linux binaries: MIT; its notice follows.
- SQLite, compiled in through rusqlite's bundled build: public domain, no
  notice needed.
- The secret scan's word list: web2 (Webster's Second International
  Dictionary, 1934), public domain, no notice needed.
- The typosquat check's popular package names (assets/defaults/popular.toml),
  from public download rankings:
  - npm: npm-high-impact (MIT); its licence follows.
  - PyPI: hugovk/top-pypi-packages, from PyPI's public download statistics.
  - crates.io: the crates.io API's download counts.
  - RubyGems: packages.ecosyste.ms, whose data is CC BY-SA 4.0
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). The RubyGems list is
    from Ecosyste.ms and is under the same licence.

The rest follow, each as its source ships it.
